##
    Sets, and the same words on a string.

    A set holds values in the order they were put there. It is not the set of
    mathematics: the order matters, and the same value may appear twice. It is a row of boxes
    to add to and take from.

    Nearly every word here also works on a string, because a string is a row of characters and
    the questions asked of a row are the same. Where the two differ is worth learning once:
    **a set changes, and a string does not.**
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.BuildingOne();
        Program.AskingAboutIt();
        Program.TakingARunOut();
        Program.TwoSetsTogether();
        Program.TheSameWordsOnAString();
        Program.AStringDoesNotChange();
        Program.BetweenTheTwo();
        Program.DroppingTheEmpties();
    end function

    # ---- Building one, and changing it ------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: Putting a set together and taking things out of it.

        @remarks: Insert, InsertAt, RemoveAt and Clear yield nothing, because they change the
        set in front of you rather than handing back a new one. Remove is the exception: it
        yields whether there was anything to remove.
    ##
    function BuildingOne()
        Console.WriteLine("== building one ==");

        integer[] numbers = {1, 2, 3};

        numbers.Insert(4);
        numbers.InsertAt(0, 0);

        Console.WriteLine("  after inserting   {{numbers}}");

        numbers.RemoveAt(0);

        Console.WriteLine("  after removing at {{numbers}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  removing a 3      {{numbers.Remove(3)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  removing a 99     {{numbers.Remove(99)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  what is left      {{numbers}}");

        # The same value may sit in a set more than once. This is why Union appends rather
        # than merging, and why these are not the operations of the same name in mathematics.
        integer[] repeated = {1, 1, 2, 2};

        Console.WriteLine("  repeats are fine  {{repeated}}");

        # Distinct is how you ask for one of each, keeping the first of every repeat so what
        # comes back is in the order the values were first met. It is the one member that
        # turns this into the set of mathematics, and it only does so when asked.
        Console.WriteLine("  one of each       {{repeated.Distinct()}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  and the original  {{repeated}}");
    end function

    # ---- Asking about it ----------------------------------------------------------------

    # @summary: Count, Contains and IndexOf, which read a set without changing it.
    function AskingAboutIt()
        Console.WriteLine("== asking ==");

        string[] shopping = {"bread", "milk", "eggs"};

        Console.WriteLine("  how many      {{shopping.Count}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  any milk      {{shopping.Contains("milk")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  where is milk {{shopping.IndexOf("milk")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  any jam       {{shopping.Contains("jam")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  where is jam  {{shopping.IndexOf("jam")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  the first one {{shopping[0]}}");
    end function

    # ---- Taking a run out -----------------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: Copying a run of a set out of the middle of it.

        @remarks: Subset copies and leaves the original alone. The second number says where to
        stop rather than how many, which is the same reading "until" has in a loop — and it is
        what makes Subset(0, n) and Subset(n, count) put the whole thing back together.
    ##
    function TakingARunOut()
        Console.WriteLine("== a run of it ==");

        integer[] all = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        Console.WriteLine("  from 2 on     {{all.Subset(2)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  from 1 to 3   {{all.Subset(1, 3)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  the two halves put back together: "
                              + "{{all.Subset(0, 2).Union(all.Subset(2, 5))}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  and the original is untouched     {{all}}");
    end function

    # ---- Two sets read together -------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: Union, Intersect and Except, which read two sets together.

        @remarks: All three hand back a new set and leave both originals alone. Union appends,
        so a value in both ends up in the answer twice — the order and the repeats are kept,
        because that is what this kind of set is.

        Intersect and Except divide a set in two between them: every element goes to exactly one
        of the answers. Putting those back together therefore returns every element — though in
        the order they were divided, not the order they started in.
    ##
    function TwoSetsTogether()
        Console.WriteLine("== two together ==");

        integer[] mine = {1, 2, 3};
        integer[] yours = {3, 4, 5};

        Console.WriteLine("  both piles     {{mine.Union(yours)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  in both        {{mine.Intersect(yours)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  only mine      {{mine.Except(yours)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  only yours     {{yours.Except(mine)}}");

        Console.WriteLine("  every element back, reordered: "
                              + "{{mine.Intersect(yours).Union(mine.Except(yours))}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  and mine is untouched {{mine}}");

        # Union followed by Distinct is the union of mathematics, said in two steps because
        # they are two decisions: put them together, then decide about the repeats.
        Console.WriteLine("  merged, not appended  {{mine.Union(yours).Distinct()}}");
    end function

    # ---- The same words, on a string ----------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: The same words asked of a string.

        @remarks: A string answers the row-of-things questions the same way, because that is
        what it is. Only the element differs: a character rather than whatever the set holds.
    ##
    function TheSameWordsOnAString()
        Console.WriteLine("== the same words on a string ==");

        let text = "Profi-C";

        Console.WriteLine("  how many        {{text.Count}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  any 'fi'        {{text.Contains("fi")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  where is 'fi'   {{text.IndexOf("fi")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  from 5 on       {{text.Subset(5)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  from 0 to 5     {{text.Subset(0, 5)}}");

        # Substring asks the other way round: how many rather than where to stop. Whichever
        # number you happen to have is the one to write.
        Console.WriteLine("  five characters {{text.Substring(0, 5)}}");
    end function

    # ---- Where the two part company ------------------------------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: Where a set and a string part company.

        @remarks: Inserting into a set changes that set. Inserting into a string cannot change
        it — a string never changes — so it hands back a new one and leaves the original
        exactly as it was.

        Everything surprising about text in most languages comes from missing this.
    ##
    function AStringDoesNotChange()
        Console.WriteLine("== a set changes, a string does not ==");

        integer[] numbers = {1, 2};
        numbers.Insert(3);

        Console.WriteLine("  the set changed      {{numbers}}");

        let greeting = "hello";
        let longer = greeting.Insert(", world");

        Console.WriteLine("  the new string       {{longer}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  the original one     {{greeting}}");

        # So every one of these hands something back, and throwing it away does nothing.
        Console.WriteLine("  without the middle   {{"a-b-c".Remove("-b")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  without the first    {{"abc".RemoveAt(0)}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  with something in it {{"ac".InsertAt(1, "b")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  trimmed              [{{"  padded  ".Trim()}}]");
    end function

    # ---- Getting from one to the other ---------------------------------------------------

    # @summary: Going from a string to a set of characters and back again.
    function BetweenTheTwo()
        Console.WriteLine("== between the two ==");

        let sentence = "the quick brown fox";

        string[] words = sentence.Split(" ");
        character[] letters = "abc".ToCharacters();

        Console.WriteLine("  split into words   {{words}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  joined again       {{words.Join(" ")}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  as characters      {{letters}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  how many words     {{words.Count}}");

        # A set of anything joins, so this works on the numbers too.
        integer[] counts = {1, 2, 3};

        Console.WriteLine("  numbers joined     {{counts.Join(" then ")}}");
    end function

    # ---- The one thing only a set of optionals answers -----------------------------------

    ##
        @summary: Dropping the gaps out of a set of optionals.

        @remarks: Trim, TrimStart and TrimEnd drop them from the ends only, so the type must go
        on saying a gap is possible. TrimAll drops every one, and so yields a set where nothing
        can be absent — which is why it is the only one that changes the type, and why nothing
        after it has to unwrap.
    ##
    function DroppingTheEmpties()
        Console.WriteLine("== dropping the gaps ==");

        integer? nothing;
        integer?[] readings = {nothing, 1, nothing, 2, nothing};

        Console.WriteLine("  as they came    {{readings}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  ends trimmed    {{readings.Trim()}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  start only      {{readings.TrimStart()}}");

        integer[] certain = readings.TrimAll();

        Console.WriteLine("  every gap gone  {{certain}}");
        Console.WriteLine("  and no unwrapping needed: {{certain[0] + certain[1]}}");
    end function

end model
